is operated** — a heading, a period label, an entry's title and a rendered body's headings are
set in Charter; every control, label and value is set in the system sans.

**Icons are outlines**, drawn on one 16×16 box at a 1.2–1.4 stroke. The single deliberate solid
is the calendar's stage square, which is its task marker filled in and would otherwise be the
same drawing as it.

**The quick row.** That a new entry is an event whose row holds `24h`, `!`, and the three field
icons, and that choosing a task adds the stage between them — the stage alone, since important is
on every entry now. That stage cycles through three stages in three colours and back, the colour
being the icon's own: a **ticked square with the tick knocked out of it**, drawn in the stage's
colour, which is what the badge on its corner used to carry. That the category's **ball is drawn
in its category's colour** on the detail line when one is picked and in the row's ink when none
is. That
`!` never light with `--good`, because green is the
disk's. That the row sits **below** the when row, not above it — resolved from the elements'
document order, since a row that merely looks right can still be the old one.

content.content:
/* ---- icon buttons ---- */
.ib {
  display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex: none;
  width: 2.1rem; height: 2.1rem; margin: 0; padding: 0;
  color: var(--fg); background: none; border: 0; border-radius: .45rem; cursor: pointer;
  transition: background .12s ease;
}
.ib svg { width: 1.1rem; height: 1.1rem; display: block; }
.ib .glyph { font-size: .9rem; font-weight: 700; line-height: 1; }
.ib[disabled] { opacity: .3; }
/* An icon whose field is set reads as enabled, and it does so **in that field's own colour**:
   the category lights in the category's colour, important in the red importance is drawn in
   everywhere else, and every other field in the one accent. `--on` is set on the button by the
   app that knows which field it is; without one the lit state falls back to the plain accent. */
.ib.on { color: var(--on-ink, var(--raise)); background: var(--on, var(--accent)); }
/* A save is a green disk and a trashcan is a red trashcan - both glyphs on no background.
   Nothing in Mine is a filled green button. */
.ib.save { color: var(--good); }
.ib.bad { color: var(--bad); }
.ib.next svg { transform: rotate(180deg); }
  .knav svg { width: 1.7rem; height: 1.7rem; }
}

/* ---- the entry popup ---- */
.iconbar {
  display: flex; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .15rem;
  padding-bottom: .5rem; margin-bottom: .5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
}
.iconbar .spacer { flex: 1; }
/* The quick row, under the when: every small value as an icon and no names, with a chevron at
   its end that shows the names as lines beneath it. It does not wrap - six icons and the
   chevron are one line at 320px - and it carries no border of its own, the rows below having
   one each. */
.quick { padding: .5rem 0 .2rem; margin: 0; border: 0; flex-wrap: nowrap; }
.quick .more svg { transform: rotate(-90deg); }
.quick .more.open svg { transform: rotate(90deg); }

/* All-day and important on one line, each an icon and its word: a yes or no is not a field, so
   neither carries a trashcan, and neither fills a row on its own. */
.chips { gap: .45rem; }
.fchip {
  display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .4rem; margin: 0; padding: .42rem .78rem;
  font: inherit; font-size: .85rem; color: var(--fg); background: none;
  border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 999px; cursor: pointer;
}
/* Lit in its own colour, like the icon that carries the same value above it - a tint rather
   than a solid, a chip having a word on it as well as a glyph. */
.fchip.on {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--on, var(--accent)) 13%, transparent);
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--on, var(--accent)) 45%, var(--line));
  color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--on, var(--accent)) 72%, var(--fg));
  font-weight: 600;
}
.fchip .glyph { font-size: .8rem; font-weight: 700; }
.btngroup { display: flex; margin: .7rem 0 0; }
.seg {
  flex: 1; width: auto; margin: 0; padding: .55rem .4rem; font-size: .85rem; font-weight: 500;
  color: var(--fg); background: var(--bg); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 0;
}
.seg:first-child { border-radius: .5rem 0 0 .5rem; }
.seg:last-child { border-radius: 0 .5rem .5rem 0; border-left: 0; }
.seg[aria-pressed="true"] { color: var(--bg); background: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent); }
/* A repeating entry's scope pair rides in the top row beside the type marker, so it hugs its two
   words instead of spanning the card the way a pair under the title did. `.iconbar` wraps, and a
   stretching pair would push the trashcan, the disk and the x onto a second line. */
.iconbar .btngroup { margin: 0 .2rem; }
.iconbar .seg { flex: none; padding: .35rem .55rem; }
.body-in {
  display: block; width: 100%; margin-top: .3rem; padding: .7rem;
  font: .85rem/1.5 var(--mono);
  color: var(--fg); background: var(--bg); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: .45rem;
  resize: vertical;
}
/* A row is the icon, then the value - no field name beside it, because the name said what the
   icon already says in the one column where the value has to fit. An unset row shows the name in
   the value's place, muted, which is what an empty input does everywhere else in Mine. */
.frow {
  display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .6rem; width: 100%; margin: 0;
  min-height: 3.25rem; padding: .55rem .3rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
}
.frow > svg { flex: none; width: 1.15rem; height: 1.15rem; }
/* The whole row left of its trashcan is the way in, so it is the button. */
.ftap {
  flex: 1; min-width: 0; margin: 0; padding: .1rem .2rem; font: inherit; font-size: .9rem;
  text-align: left; color: var(--fg); background: none; border: 0; border-radius: .4rem;
  overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap; cursor: pointer;
}
.ftap:hover { background: var(--soft); }
.ftap.unset { color: var(--muted); }
/* An inline row: the input takes the line the value was on, with the trashcan and the x where the
   closed row's trashcan already is. The row's min-height above is what holds its height still, and
   it is set by the tallest thing a row can hold - its trashcan and its x, which a set row already
   carries shut. Both the text a row shows shut and the input it holds open are shorter than that,
// on a span, which is what both abbreviations are for.
export const shortDayLabel = key =>
  `${weekdayName(key, 'short').replace(/\./g, '').toUpperCase()}, ${shortDate(key, S.prefs.dateFormat)}`;

// Text on a group-coloured background. Luminance rather than a fixed colour, because the
// groups are user-chosen and a light one would otherwise carry white text.
export function inkOn(hex) {
  const h = String(hex).replace('#', '');
  const n = h.length === 3 ? h.split('').map(c => c + c).join('') : h.slice(0, 6);
  const [r, g, b] = [0, 2, 4].map(i => parseInt(n.slice(i, i + 2), 16) / 255 || 0);
  const lum = 0.2126 * r + 0.7152 * g + 0.0722 * b;
  return lum > 0.6 ? '#1b1f24' : '#fff';
}

// The inclusive day range the current view shows.
export function viewRange() {
  const { mode, anchor, sprint } = S.view;
  if (mode === 'D') return [anchor, anchor];
  if (mode === 'W') { const a = weekStartOf(anchor, S.prefs.weekStart); return [a, addDays(a, 6)]; }
  if (mode === 'M') {
    const q = indexedDB.open('mine', 1);
    q.onsuccess = () => {
      const t = q.result.transaction('files', 'readonly').objectStore('files').getAll();
      t.onsuccess = () => r(t.result);
    };
  });
  const f = all.find(f => (f.text || '').includes('Water the plants'));
  return f ? f.text : 'gone';
})()`);
const label = g => g.split('@')[0].split('[')[0];
const badge = g => (g.match(/\[(.*)\]/) || [, ''])[1];

// A row: the field it is, the text it shows, whether that text is the muted field name rather
#!/bin/bash
# Runs the model checks in node, then starts the fixture server and a headless Chromium for the
# browser checks and tears both down.
#
#   test/run.sh              every check
#   test/run.sh --shot DIR   every check, and a screenshot of each view into DIR
#   test/run.sh mine         one app's browser checks, by the name in test/check-<app>.mjs
#
# Needs chromium and node 20+ on PATH. Nothing is installed and nothing is written to the repo.
#
# A browser suite per app, discovered rather than listed: adding test/check-diary.mjs is all it
# takes for the diary to be checked by this script. They share one Chromium and run in turn,
# because they drive the same page target.
set -u
HERE=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
PORT=8787
PROFILE=$(mktemp -d)

cleanup() {
  [ -n "${CHROME:-}" ] && kill "$CHROME" 2>/dev/null
  [ -n "${SERVER:-}" ] && kill "$SERVER" 2>/dev/null
  rm -rf "$PROFILE" 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}
trap cleanup EXIT

command -v chromium >/dev/null || { echo "chromium not on PATH"; exit 2; }

# A bare word is an app name; everything else is passed to the suites.
ONLY=""
ARGS=()
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --shot) ARGS+=("$1" "$2"); shift 2 ;;
    -*) ARGS+=("$1"); shift ;;
    *) ONLY="$1"; shift ;;
  esac
done

SUITES=()

